# NUMERICS: `%` on two integers is FLOORED — the remainder takes the DIVISOR's
# sign — and it does so whatever shape the divisor arrives in.
#
# THE RULING CHANGED AND THIS FILE CHANGED WITH IT, 2026-08-15. This header used
# to read "`%` … TRUNCATES … §12 B-4 says `i64/i64` truncates … `%` matches",
# and it pinned `-1 1 -1 1 -1 1`. **`§12 B-4` does not exist.** It is quoted
# verbatim by six files in this corpus and by `docs/spec/cost.md:356`, and the
# document it names is in neither tree — the same class of defect as the four
# phantom law ids in `docs/law-audit.md` §1.7. One of its four clauses is also
# checkably FALSE about this language: it says "no `//`", and `//` is lexed
# (`lexer.zig:1018`), parsed (`parser.zig:5388`), given a precedence row and a
# formatter spelling.
#
# `docs/rulings.md` § "Modulo and floor division" settles it from `law.md`,
# which IS in the tree and IS supreme: its first line makes "ordinary Lua
# meaning" the entry of the specialization chain, and Lua's `%` is floored.
# An `i64` descriptor cannot be the excuse — Lua's `%` already answers an
# INTEGER for two integers, so the descriptor changes nothing about it.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FILE IS STILL THE DIVISOR'S SHAPE, and that is why it
# survived the ruling instead of being replaced. Every row below is the same
# arithmetic reached through a different divisor shape — positive literal,
# negative literal, and a value the emitter cannot read — so a rule that holds
# for one shape and not another cannot pass. Under the repair those shapes take
# three different machine paths (`and #imm`, the constant-divisor correction,
# and `emitFlooredDivRem`), which makes this file MORE load-bearing than before.
#
# Computed by hand from Lua 5.4 §3.4.1, `a % b == a - floor(a/b)*b`:
#   -7 % 2 = -7 - (-4 * 2) = 1.   7 % -2 = 7 - (-4 * -2) = -1.
# expect: 1 -1 1 -1 1 -1
# @corpus current
two = 2
neg = 0 - two
a = -7 % 2
b = 7 % -2
c = -7 % two
d = 7 % neg
e = 0 - 7
f = e % two
g = 7 % neg
print("{a} {b} {c} {d} {f} {g}")
0
